                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "**Extractability in AI search** is how easily an AI system can isolate a clear, self-contained passage from a webpage and use it in an answer or citation.[1][2]\n\nIt differs from **retrieval**:\n\n- **Retrieval:** AI finds and considers your page.\n- **Extractability:** AI can understand and reuse a specific passage from that page without needing surrounding context.[2][6]\n\nHighly extractable content typically:\n\n- Gives the direct answer early.\n- Uses short, precise, unambiguous sentences.\n- Names the subject instead of relying on pronouns like \u201cit\u201d or \u201cthey.\u201d\n- Makes each section understandable on its own.\n- Uses descriptive headings, lists, tables, or FAQs.\n- States claims explicitly rather than requiring interpretation.[3][8]\n\n**Example**\n\nLess extractable:  \n\u201cBecause of this, it can be particularly useful in certain situations.\u201d\n\nMore extractable:  \n\u201cExtractability helps AI search systems reuse a passage because the passage states its subject and meaning independently.\u201d\n\nIn practical terms, extractability helps turn a page that is merely **found** into one that is **quoted or cited** in an AI-generated answer.[2][7]",
